# Layer 2 — Full Document: SL-OVERVIEW
# SIGN Language — Specification & Parser Overview

@doc SL-OVERVIEW [ref, active, agents+employees] v1 sha:pending
@links SL-ARCH,SL-DEPS,SL-STANDARDS

@anchor [repo:sign-lang, vcs:git]
  + spec/**
  + README.md
  + CONTRIBUTING.md
  + examples/**
  + decisions/**
  ! **/*.test.js

@def sign-lang
  Open-source specification and toolchain for SIGN (Sigil Intelligence Graph Notation) v1.0.
  SIGN is a strongly typed, human-readable, token-optimized notation language designed for
  the agentic consumer. It is the contract layer between a governed knowledge graph and the
  AI agents that consume it. Every design decision follows from one constraint: the LLM
  agent context window is the primary unit of consumption.

@props
  + token-efficiency      | 50-61% token reduction over equivalent JSON
  + two-layer-delivery    | Layer 1 index (~400 tokens, always injected) + Layer 2 full docs (on-demand)
  + sigil-semantics       | @-prefixed structural primitives agents parse by pattern recognition
  + self-describing       | an agent encountering SIGN for the first time infers structure from sigils alone
  + governance-native     | lifecycle status, versioning, review cycles, provenance built in
  + relationship-model    | schema-level (@rel) and instance-level (@edges) with full provenance
  + inference-rules       | @infer derives new facts from graph state with confidence and source
  + constraint-system     | mutex, requires, forbids — structural rules agents enforce
  + cluster-semantics     | named typed skill sets with threshold (capability, co-occurrence, transition, AI impact)
  + vocabulary-governance | @vocab makes the relationship graph self-describing

@boundary
  SIGN is not a general-purpose serialization format. It does not compete with JSON.
  JSON serializes data for machines; SIGN declares knowledge for agents. The notation
  targets a different consumer: the LLM attention mechanism operating within a token budget.
  The toolchain (validator, compiler, coverage, drift, diff) is open-source under Apache 2.0.
  The spec lives at spec/sign-v1.0.md; the sigil quick-reference at spec/sigil-reference.md.

@status
  specVersion:          v1.0
  specDate:             April 2026
  license:              Apache-2.0
  origin:               Career Highways
  doi:                  10.17605/OSF.IO/6YZJ7
